WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.375
Scenario: A staff member with legitimate access downloads an

2
00:00:03.375 --> 00:00:07.500
unusually large volume of project files outside their normal work pattern.

3
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.625
There is no evidence yet of data exfiltration or malicious intent.

4
00:00:11.625 --> 00:00:15.000
Safest response: Option A: Preserve relevant authorized telemetry and

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.375
route the observable anomaly to the designated security/insider-risk process

6
00:00:18.375 --> 00:00:21.750
for proportionate review. Focus on verifiable behavior, policy and

7
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:25.125
authorized investigation, not unsupported personal assumptions. Option B is

8
00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:28.500
unsafe because Unapproved surveillance or access can violate law,

9
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.875
policy and privacy; follow authorized procedures. Option C is

10
00:00:31.875 --> 00:00:35.250
unsafe because Anomalous activity is not proof of intent;

11
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.625
public accusation can harm fairness and compromise an investigation.

12
00:00:38.625 --> 00:00:42.000
Option D is unsafe because Authorized access can still

13
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.750
be misused or become anomalous; the activity may warrant review.

14
00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:49.125
In practice: Preserve relevant logs according to policy, report

15
00:00:49.125 --> 00:00:52.500
through designated channels, limit information to need-to-know reviewers and

16
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.875
involve HR/legal/privacy as required. Key takeaway: An indicator is

17
00:00:55.875 --> 00:00:59.250
a reason for proportionate review—not a diagnosis of motive

18
00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.000
or guilt.
